# Stable names for the rig's USB serial devices.
#
#     sudo cp scripts/gnss/99-rig-serial.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#     sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL ANY MORE. The IMU bridge hardcodes /dev/ttyACM0
# (launch/rig.launch.py, ros2/imu_bridge_node/imu_bridge_node.py). The LG290P
# enumerates as a CH343 USB-serial and takes /dev/ttyACM0 too -- verified
# 2026-08-06, when it grabbed ACM0 with the XIAO unplugged. With both
# attached, whichever enumerates first wins, and the loser silently gets the
# wrong device: the bridge would try to parse NMEA as IMU packets, or the
# NTRIP client would write RTCM into the IMU.
#
# Matching on idVendor/idProduct alone is not enough here -- 1a86:55d3 is a
# generic QinHeng CH343, not specific to this module -- so the serial number
# pins it.

# u-blox... no: Quectel LG290P behind a CH343 bridge. Verified by udevadm.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="55d3", ATTRS{serial}=="5B90166916", SYMLINK+="gnss", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

# XIAO ESP32-S3 (IMU bridge). NOT YET FILLED IN -- the board was unplugged
# when this file was written, so its idVendor/idProduct/serial have not been
# read from the device and MUST NOT be guessed. With it plugged in:
#
#     udevadm info -a -n /dev/ttyACM0 | grep -E 'ATTRS\{(idVendor|idProduct|serial)\}' | head -3
#
# then uncomment and fill:
# SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="XXXX", ATTRS{idProduct}=="XXXX", ATTRS{serial}=="XXXXXXXX", SYMLINK+="imu", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"
